{
  "index": 3,
  "response": " The North Atlantic Treaty, signed on April 4, 1949, outlines a mutual defense agreement between its member states for collective security and peaceful conflict resolution. Here's a summary of the key articles:\n\n1. Article 1: Parties commit to resolving international disputes peacefully, refraining from using force inconsistent with the United Nations' purposes, and aiming to maintain international peace and security and justice.\n\n2. Article 2: Parties pledge to develop friendly international relations by strengthening their free institutions, fostering mutual understanding, and promoting stability and well-being, also seeking to eliminate conflicts in economic policies and encouraging economic collaboration.\n\n3. Article 3: Parties aim to effectively achieve the treaty's objectives by maintaining and developing their individual and collective capacity to resist armed attacks through continuous self-help and mutual aid.\n\n4. Article 5: In case of an armed attack against any party in Europe or North America, it will be considered an attack against all parties. Each party agrees to assist the attacked party/parties using necessary actions, including armed force, to restore and maintain the security of the North Atlantic area under the right of individual or collective self-defense recognized by Article 51 of the United Nations Charter.",
